A||||6||prof bobo||MPFI fuel pressure regulator Q||||1||
Z||000000||prof bobo||04-23-2001||01:42 PM||||Say, what is the purpose of the vacuum connection to the fuel pressure regulator anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||04-23-2001||03:00 PM||||Think the regulator tries to keep fuel pressure in the fuel rail a given pressure above manifold absolute pressure.  So - with increased engine loads, you get lower vacuum, therefore higher manifold absolute pressure, and thus higher absolute fuel pressure.<P>Another well-known, brilliant scientifical principle I just made up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Either that, or just say it increases fuel pressure under low-vacuum (high-load) conditions.     ||||reg||3
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||04-23-2001||09:16 PM||||The vacuum takes the pressure off of the spring that holds the return line and raises the pressure. At idle, the high vacuum is causing the FPR to not restrict the fuel line and keep the pressure low. When you step on the gas, the vacuum drops and the spring blocks off the return line and raises the fuel pressure.<P>Or it uses some sort of gypsy magic, I'm not entirely sure.||||reg||1
Z||000003||prof bobo||04-24-2001||10:34 AM||||So ... it's like the 'accelerator pump' on a carburator?  One would think that the ECM could derive 'high load' given MAP, TPS, and engine RPM and just increase the pulse width and frequency of the injectors to compensate.  I wonder if the vacuum modulated fuel regulator was just a convenient reuse of old technology or a deliberate design decision (ECM unable to detect and compensate in the required amount of time?)||||reg||1
Z||000004||gixxer||04-24-2001||11:09 AM||||It keeps the pressure differential across the injectors the same.<BR>Example:<BR>at full throttle the injectors see 44 PSI at the fuel supply side, and at the intake manifold side see about zero vacuum (atmospheric),for a net difference of 44PSI.<BR>If you were to lift off the throttle, the injectors (without the regulator)would see 44PSI and the intake manifold would be about 14 PSI VACUUM for a net pressure differential of 58 PSI.<BR>The engine would then run rich. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||04-24-2001||02:43 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by prof bobo:<BR><B>So ... it's like the 'accelerator pump' on a carburator?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nope - more like an altitude compensator/ power valve typa thing on a carburetor, if anything.<P>Sorry, I know that doesn't help   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> but I really don't think accelerator pump is it at all.  I like gixxer's answer best.<p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||gixxer||04-24-2001||06:11 PM||||Gixxer stole answer from factory shop manual. =)<BR>Make Gixxer sound smart! ;P<BR>Heh.||||reg||1
